### 1. What [`AddFreightRate()`](file:///c:/Users/krithika/Documents/Testing/Cathy_Angular_Portfolio/Class5_Ardent_FreightRate_Dropdown.txt#L79-L85) Is Doing Right Now:
Right now, in your file ([`Class5_Ardent_FreightRate_Dropdown.txt`](file:///c:/Users/krithika/Documents/Testing/Cathy_Angular_Portfolio/Class5_Ardent_FreightRate_Dropdown.txt#L83)), this line:
```typescript
this.result = 'Added Rate: ' + this.shipFrom + ' -> ' + this.shipTo + ' (' + this.mode + ') at $' + this.oct26;
```
is **only displaying that 1 line of text on the screen** inside `<h3>{{ result }}</h3>`.
- It is **not** saving into SQL Server (since you don't have SQL database access on your laptop).
- And because `this.result` is just **one single text variable**, if you add `1002 - ALTON IL` first and then click `Add` for `1001 - ALBANY NY`, the second one **replaces** the first one on the screen!

---

### 2. Can We Make It Actually ADD to a Growing List/Table on the Screen? (YES — in 3 Lines!)
In Java, when you want to keep adding rows into memory so they stack up in a table, you use `ArrayList.add(...)`.
In TypeScript / Angular, an array list uses **`.push(...)`**!

Look how cool this is — if you add **1 array variable** (`savedRatesList: any = [];`) and **1 line inside `AddFreightRate()`**:

```typescript
  savedRatesList: any = [];   // <-- 1. Starts as an empty list []

  AddFreightRate() {
    if (this.shipFrom == '' || this.shipTo == '' || this.mode == '') {
      this.result = 'Error: Please select Ship From, Ship To, and Mode!';
    } else {
      this.result = 'Added Rate: ' + this.shipFrom + ' -> ' + this.shipTo + ' (' + this.mode + ') at $' + this.oct26;
      this.savedRatesList.push(this.result);   // <-- 2. Adds each new rate into the list!
    }
  }
```
And then at the bottom of your HTML `template`, you use the exact same `@for` loop you already learned for your dropdowns to display every saved row:
```html
    <h4>Saved Freight Rates Table:</h4>
    @for (row of savedRatesList; track row) {
      <p>{{ row }}</p>
    }
```

